  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Wildlife Without Borders-Western Hemisphere</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Located in the Selva Lacandona, the Sierra Cojolita Communal Reserve is a unique example of community conservation. Established in 1990 by the Mexican government, the Reserve is own by three indigenous groups (Lacandon, Chol and Tzeltal). It has an extension of 34,000 has of untouched low-land forest and a unique biodiversity richness (one of the last viable populations of jaguars, white lipped peccaries, and tapirs in Middle America) that is now threatened with agricultural expansion, forest clearing for cropland and pasture, and pressure from human migration and illegal settlement patterns. This project will build the capacity of the three indigenous communities to design a REDD+ (Reduced Emissions from Deforestation and Degradation) pilot project that will not only protect one of Mexicos most biodiverse and critically threatened regions, but that is also socially equitable and economically sustainable. Project activities include: 1) implementation of the second phase of the Free, Prior, and Informed Consent (FPIC) process; 2) implementation of 7-8 workshops (serving at least 500 people) on REDD+ information, social biodiversity impact assessment, conflict mediation/GPS training and participatory mapping, analysis and strategies to past barriers to conservation, and 3) develop a communal land use management plan that supports the long-term socio economic and environmental sustainability of the Reserve.</narrative>
  </description>
